crypto/tls: add Config.SignatureSchemes

crypto/tls advertises a fixed list of signature algorithms in the
signature_algorithms and signature_algorithms_cert extensions of the
ClientHello and the CertificateRequest. A TLS 1.3 peer whose
certificate key is on a Brainpool curve must sign with
ecdsa_brainpoolP256r1tls13_sha256, ecdsa_brainpoolP384r1tls13_sha384 or
ecdsa_brainpoolP512r1tls13_sha512 (RFC 8734), so it can only use a
certificate, or be accepted, if the other side lists those algorithms.
Products in the German health care network must also restrict the
advertised algorithms (no Ed25519 or ML-DSA, BSI TR-02102-2).

Add Config.SignatureSchemes, shaped like the field requested in
golang/go#63527: the algorithms to advertise and accept, in preference
order, instead of the default list. Algorithms that are disabled for
the protocol versions offered, in FIPS 140-3 mode or by GODEBUG settings
are left out of signature_algorithms, as for the default list; the
signature_algorithms_cert list keeps the ones disabled only for
handshake signatures. Algorithms this package doesn't implement are
advertised only for TLS 1.3 and only if VerifyUnsupportedSignature is
set: only peers whose keys this package doesn't support can use them,
and the function verifies them. The field doesn't restrict the
algorithms used for signing; Certificate.SupportedSignatureAlgorithms
does that.

The field is provisional: if golang/go#63527 adds such a list, this
change reduces to allowing algorithms this package doesn't implement in
it when VerifyUnsupportedSignature is set.

ivory: P5, profiles core and kt. Base: go1.27.2 with P0-P4.
Upstream: via golang/go#63527 (provisional). Requirement: A_18467
(gemSpec_Krypt V2.51.0).
Status: TLS 1.3; provisional API.

